Step 3: Using the touch command
The touch command creates an empty file.

Command 1: Create a file
touch student.txt


Expected output:
(No output)


Command 2: Verify the file
ls


Expected output (example):
Desktop  Documents  Downloads  student.txt


Command 3: Check file details
ls -l student.txt


Expected output (example):
-rw-r--r-- 1 kali kali 0 Nov 27 23:10 student.txt


Notice that the file size is 0 bytes because it is empty.

Command 4: Create multiple files
touch file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt


Verify:
ls file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt


Expected output:
file1.txt  file2.txt  file3.txt


Step 4: Using the wget command
The wget command downloads files from the internet or an accessible web server.

For this practical, we will download an approved test file.

Command 1: Download a test file
wget https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt


Expected output (example):
--2026-11-27 23:10:00--  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt
Resolving www.gnu.org...
Connecting to www.gnu.org...
HTTP request sent, awaiting response... 200 OK
Length: ... 
Saving to: 'gpl-3.0.txt'

gpl-3.0.txt       100%[==================>] 

' gpl-3.0.txt' saved


The actual download size and speed may differ.

Command 2: Verify downloaded file
ls


Expected output (example):
Desktop
Documents
Downloads
student.txt
file1.txt
file2.txt
file3.txt
gpl-3.0.txt


Command 3: Display downloaded file information
ls -lh gpl-3.0.txt


Expected output:
-rw-r--r-- 1 kali kali 35K Nov 27 23:10 gpl-3.0.txt


Command 4: Display first 10 lines
head -10 gpl-3.0.txt
